//! The page's prose, for a reader with new eyes. `[shown|id]` marks a
//! word that opens its plain explanation (docs/glossary.md); `**words**`
//! is bold. A test checks every mark names an idea and no sentence
//! passes a verdict. The numbers quoted are the run's, which is
//! deterministic; the tables beside them are read from the run itself.

pub const LEDE: &str = "Three small data sets built with a known structure, three spaces to keep them in. Part 1: the same points kept flat and as directions on the unit sphere, on flat clusters and on directions: three distances between the same pairs, a shortest path and a mean in each space, nearest neighbors and the decision boundary under each distance, a small classifier trained on each representation, and how badly each keeps the data's own distances. Part 2: the Poincare disk, a space with room to spare, and a tree fitted in the plane, on the sphere and in the disk from the same start, with curvature as a control. The test of H1.";

pub const HINT: &str = "New to this? A word with a dotted underline opens a plain explanation when you click it (or press Enter on it); click again to close it. The pictures of the sphere turn when you drag them, press the arrow keys once they have the focus, or swipe. The buttons choose which representation or curvature a picture shows; turning and choosing change no number. Part 2 (the disk and the tree) runs after part 1 has appeared and takes some seconds.";

pub const QUESTION: [&str; 3] = [
    "Does it matter what shape of space a model keeps its points in?",
    "A model keeps each thing it sees as a list of numbers, a point. Those numbers can be read as a position in a flat room, where the distance between two points is the straight line; as a direction, a point on the skin of a ball, where the distance is the angle; or as a point inside a disk whose edge is infinitely far away, where there is more and more room the farther out you go. The three readings put the same numbers in different spaces, and a method that works from distances, nearest neighbors first of all, gives different answers under each.",
    "The worry behind the question is that the choice is usually made by habit: the straight line because it is the default, the angle because word vectors are compared that way. This page makes the choice on purpose, on data whose structure is known, and measures what each choice costs and gains.",
];

pub const BACKGROUND: [&str; 7] = [
    "A [representation|representation] is the way a point is kept: the list of numbers, and the space those numbers are read in. In part 1 each point is kept two ways. Flat: as it is, in a room of two or three coordinates, with the straight line as its distance. Spherical: as its direction only, the point scaled to length 1 so that it lies on the unit sphere, with the angle between two points, the [great-circle distance|great-circle], as its distance. Both have the same [ambient dimension|ambient-dimension]; the sphere has one less [intrinsic dimension|intrinsic-dimension], since every length on it is 1. (In the plane the unit sphere is the unit circle; the page says sphere for both.)",
    "Two data sets of the experiment kit, the same ones the baseline page ran, each with a known structure. **Flat clusters**: three clusters of 40 points in the plane, scattered about centers a few units apart (the spread, 1.6, is how far a point is scattered from its center); what tells the clusters apart is where they are, so the straight line is the distance they were built for. **Directions**: four classes of 40 points in three dimensions that differ by which way they point and not by how far out they sit; each point is its class's direction pushed by 0.6 of a random vector (the cone), so two points of one class can be far apart in angle and the classes overlap at their edges; their lengths, from 0.5 to 2, were drawn at random and carry nothing. The angle is the distance they were built for. Angles here are in radians: a quarter turn is pi / 2, about 1.57; a half turn pi, about 3.14.",
    "[Nearest neighbors|nearest-neighbors] keeps the training points and answers for a new point with the label most of its k nearest training points have, under whichever distance is chosen; it learns no number. A [held-out|held-out] quarter of each set, chosen by a seed, is set aside before anything is trained and the share of it labeled right is the accuracy. The [decision boundary|decision-boundary] of a classifier is where its answer changes, drawn by asking it at every point of a grid.",
    "The **classifier** of the training run is a small network: the point's coordinates are multiplied by a matrix of weights (with one extra row, the bias, added to every input), each of the 8 results is squashed into -1 to 1 by the tanh function (a hidden unit), and a second matrix turns those 8 numbers into one score per class, which softmax turns into probabilities that sum to 1. It is trained by lowering the [cross-entropy loss|cross-entropy] on the training rows: the gradient of the loss by every weight is found by backpropagation (the chain rule applied layer by layer, from the output back), and Adam steps each weight against its gradient by a learning rate, 0.02, that sets how far each step goes. The same starting weights, steps and rate on each representation of a set. The record's operations column counts multiply-adds, as the baseline page's does: one distance per held-out point per training point for nearest neighbors; two per trained number per training row per step for the network.",
    "[Distortion|distortion] measures how badly a representation keeps a set of distances: over every pair of points, the fraction by which the representation's distance is too long or too short, averaged and at worst. Here the distances to keep are each set's own, the structure it was built with: the straight line in the plane for the clusters, the angle for the directions, and for the tree the number of edges between two nodes, its [tree distance|tree-distance].",
    "The third space is the [Poincare disk|poincare-disk]: the points inside the unit circle, measured so that a step near the rim counts for far more than the same step near the middle, by 2 / (1 - r^2) at plain radius r. Its [curvature|curvature] is -1 everywhere (the sphere's is +1, the plane's 0): triangles' angles add to less than a half turn, and a circle of radius L has length 2 pi sinh L, which doubles about every 0.7 of radius. The rim is infinitely far from every point and no path reaches it; the program keeps every point under the radius 1 - 0.00001, as the papers it follows do. The third data set is the kit's **tree**: depth 4 and branching 2, 31 nodes and 30 edges, its distances the path lengths along it (1 to 8), each node's class the branch of the root it hangs from.",
    "An [embedding|embedding] here is a fit: the tree's nodes put into a space as points and moved, step by step, so that the distances between the points along the space come as close as can be to the tree's. The step is gradient descent, each node moved a little against the gradient of the distortion, along the space: in the plane a plain step; on the sphere and in the disk the space's own step (the [exponential map|exponential-map]), which stays on the sphere or inside the disk, against the [gradient along the space|riemannian-gradient] (on the sphere the tangent part of the plain gradient; in the disk the plain gradient scaled down by the [metric|metric], the rule for how long a step counts, which near the rim counts a short step as long). A [tangent vector|tangent-vector] is a direction one can move in from a point without leaving the space: along the plane anywhere, along the sphere's skin, anywhere inside the disk. A fit can stop in a [local minimum|local-minimum], so it is run from three seeded starts and the spread between them is reported. The classifiers on an embedding are nearest neighbors under the space's own distance and a [logistic classifier|logistic-classifier] on the embedded coordinates, the simplest trained one: one score per class, a weighted sum of the coordinates plus a bias, softmax into probabilities, the weights set by gradient descent on the cross-entropy. The hyperbolic functions the disk's formulas use: sinh x = (e^x - e^-x) / 2, which grows like e^x / 2; cosh x = (e^x + e^-x) / 2; tanh x = sinh x / cosh x, between -1 and 1; asinh, acosh and atanh undo them. The 31 nodes make 465 pairs (31 times 30, halved), the pairs every distortion is averaged over.",
];

pub const RATIONALE: [&str; 3] = [
    "Hold everything equal but the space. The same points, the same split into training and held-out rows, the same seeds, the same k for nearest neighbors, the same network, starting weights, step count and learning rate for the classifier; then read each point flat and on the sphere, and measure; for the tree, the same start, steps and rate in each of the three spaces. Whatever moves between the columns is the geometry's doing, because nothing else was allowed to move.",
    "The three data sets were built so that each space has a set that matches it: the flat clusters are made of positions, the directions of angles, the tree of a hierarchy, whose levels double as the disk's room does. H1, the [hypothesis|hypothesis] of this page, says the matching space does at least as well and the crossed spaces cost something; what would count against it, written before the run: the sphere scoring as high as the plane on the clusters, or the plane as high as the sphere on the directions; and for the tree, taken from docs/research-questions.md as written before this saga: the disk counts for H1 if its distortion is under the plane's by more than the spread of the disk's own distortion over three starts (the difference between the two spaces on the first start, against the largest less the smallest of the disk's three ends) and its accuracy is at least the plane's; the plane within that spread, or above the disk on accuracy, counts against. That rule reads a tie on accuracy as for H1 where part 1's rule read a tie against; both readings are given in the analysis. The rule was written for starts compared unpaired; the design pairs them (the same seed in every space), and the paired reading is reported beside the rule's. A [null result|null-result] on any set is reported as one.",
    "For the tree the fit is at a fixed scale: an edge should come out 1 in every space. At a fixed scale the curvature means something (the same tree at the same size in a smaller or a larger space); with the scale free every curvature of one sign is the same space, and a first run with the scale free saw the sphere's fit shrink its embedding without end toward the plane's, which the control below measures instead of running away with.",
];

pub const PURPOSE: [&str; 2] = [
    "This page is the comparison of H1 in two parts. Part 1: two geometries by two data sets, with the baseline page's nearest-neighbor rows as the row every number is set against (0.900 on the clusters; 0.750 on the directions, 0.775 with their lengths dropped), and an actual training run, small, on each representation. Part 2: the Poincare disk, the tree fitted in three spaces from the same start by the same steps, three starts for the spread, and the same fit at eight curvatures, with the baseline page's plain layout of the tree (distortion 0.18 on average, 0.80 at worst) as its row against. Every point drawn, every distance, path, mean, boundary cell, loss, embedding, distortion and accuracy comes from the two X_eTaL programs at the bottom, run in your browser when the page opened. The pictures are marked [measured or analytical|measured-analytical]; all of them here are measured.",
    "It is not a proof that one space is right: three small sets; in part 1 one run per cell (one split, one set of starting weights), in part 2 three starts of a fit of 31 nodes in dimension 2, each 200 steps. The H1 table has seven cells of nine: the clusters and the directions were not fitted in the disk (120 or 160 points make 15 or 27 times the tree's pairs, so the nine fits would take minutes in a page, not seconds). And the classifiers are not trained models of anything outside this page; they are the smallest runs that can show convergence and held-out accuracy under each space.",
];

pub const INTUITION: [&str; 3] = [
    "A sheet of paper and a globe. On the sheet, the distance between two cities is the ruler's; two cities on the same street and two across town are told apart by how far the ruler stretches. On the globe, every city is a direction from the center, and the distance is how far round you must turn to look from one to the other; a city twice as far from the center in the same direction is the same city. Keep positions on the sheet and the globe throws away what tells them apart. Keep directions on the globe and the sheet's ruler adds in the lengths, which say nothing.",
    "The ambient dimension is the room's: three numbers per point in both pictures of the directions. The intrinsic dimension is the space's own: three for the room, two for the sphere's skin. Putting a point on the sphere spends one dimension, the length, on purpose. On data whose length carries nothing that dimension was noise; on data whose position carries everything it was signal.",
    "Now a family tree, drawn on a sheet. Each generation doubles, and each child should sit one step from its parent and far from its cousins; by the fifth generation the sheet has run out of room at the edge (this page's tree stops at the fourth, where the count below still finds room; what the plane loses on it is subtler, and measured, not counted), and cousins who are eight steps apart along the tree sit side by side. The disk is a sheet whose edge is infinitely far away and whose room doubles with every step outward, as the generations do: the tree fits with its distances kept. The sphere is the opposite, a sheet that closes up on itself, with less room the farther out you go and no two points more than a half turn apart: the tree's farthest leaves cannot get far enough from each other.",
];

pub const MATHEMATICS: [&str; 8] = [
    "**Three distances.** For two points p and q with coordinates p_i and q_i: the straight line is sqrt(sum of (p_i - q_i)^2). Their dot product is sum of p_i q_i, and their lengths |p| and |q| are the straight line from the origin to each. The cosine of the angle between them is p . q / (|p| |q|); the [cosine distance|cosine-distance] is 1 minus it; the angle itself, the great-circle distance between the two directions, is acos of it, in radians, 0 to pi. The program finds the angle another way (2 atan2 of |u - v| and |u + v| for the unit points u and v, which loses no digits near 0 or pi) and checks the two agree.",
    "**Shortest paths.** In the flat space the shortest path from p to q is the segment, p + t (q - p) for t from 0 to 1. On the sphere it is the arc of the great circle through the two unit points, which the program draws as 13 points along it; the chord between the same two points, straight through the inside, is 2 sin(angle / 2), shorter than the arc, which is the angle.",
    "**Means.** The plain mean of n points is their coordinate-by-coordinate average; for points on the sphere it falls inside the sphere, since averaging directions shortens them. The [mean on the sphere|frechet-mean] is the point of the sphere whose summed squared distances along the sphere to the points is least, found by stepping along the average of the arrows from the current guess to the points until the arrows cancel.",
    "**Nearest neighbors under the angle.** For unit points, the chord between two is 2 sin(angle / 2), which only grows with the angle, so the straight line between unit points and the angle order every point's neighbors the same way; the program checks this on all 160 unit points. So nearest neighbors under the angle is nearest neighbors by the straight line on the unit points, and that is how it is run. The cosine distance orders them the same way too.",
    "**The classifier and its loss.** With x a point's coordinates, W1 a 3 by 8 (or 4 by 8) matrix whose last row is a bias and W2 a 9 by 3 (or 9 by 4) one: h = tanh(x W1), the hidden layer; z = h W2; the probabilities are softmax(z), each exp(z_c) over their sum. The loss is the cross-entropy, minus the mean over training rows of the log of the true class's probability. The gradient of the loss by W1 and W2 is written out by the Net library (backpropagation) and a test checks it against central differences: (loss(w + h) - loss(w - h)) / 2h at five weights, h = 0.00001. Adam steps each weight by the learning rate times a running average of its gradient divided by the square root of a running average of its square.",
    "**The disk's distance.** For two points u and v inside the unit disk, with |u - v| the plain distance between them: d(u, v) = 2 asinh(|u - v| / sqrt((1 - |u|^2) (1 - |v|^2))), the same as acosh(1 + 2 |u - v|^2 / ((1 - |u|^2) (1 - |v|^2))), the form Nickel and Kiela write; the program takes the asinh form, which subtracts nothing from 1 but the two squared lengths, and checks the two agree. From the middle to a point at plain radius r it is 2 atanh r. The shortest path, the [geodesic|geodesic], is an arc of a circle that meets the rim at right angles, or a diameter; the program draws it by Mobius addition, the disk's own way of adding, u (+) v = ((1 + 2 u . v + |v|^2) u + (1 - |u|^2) v) / (1 + 2 u . v + |u|^2 |v|^2), which stays inside the disk and is plain addition near the middle.",
    "**The step along each space.** A fit moves every node against the gradient of the loss, the mean over pairs of (E / D - 1)^2 with E the distance along the space and D the tree's. The gradient by the distances is a matrix W (2 (E / D - 1) / D over the pairs); the gradient by the points is, for node i, the sum over j of W_ij times the gradient of d(P_i, P_j) by P_i: in the plane (P_i - P_j) / |P_i - P_j|; on the sphere -P_j / sin(angle), then the tangent part at P_i; in the disk the gradient of 2 asinh z scaled by (1 - |P_i|^2)^2 / 4, the metric's correction, which makes a step near the rim small. The step: P - lr G in the plane; on the sphere cos(t) P + sin(t) G / t with t = lr |G|, which stays on the sphere; in the disk Mobius addition of P and tanh(lambda |v| / 2) v / |v| with v = -lr G and lambda = 2 / (1 - |P|^2), which stays inside, then the clamp to radius 1 - 0.00001 with how far any point went past it kept as the constraint's violation. Every gradient is checked against central differences: the library's against its distances (at the middle of the disk and near its rim), and the program's whole loss, in the plane and in the disk, at h = 0.00001.",
    "**Curvature as a scale.** A sphere of curvature K > 0 is the unit sphere with every length times its radius 1 / sqrt K; a disk of curvature K < 0 is the unit disk with every length divided by sqrt -K. So the fit at curvature K keeps its points in the unit space and scales their distances, and a step at rate lr along the space of curvature K is the unit space's step at rate lr sqrt |K| with the same W: the scaling of the distances up and of the coordinates down cancel in the gradient, and only the step's length is left scaled. As K goes to 0 from either side the fit goes to the plane's.",
];

pub const EXPERIMENT: [&str; 5] = [
    "Two sets by two representations, four cells; on each cell two methods, nearest neighbors (k = 5) and the network. The flat clusters: seed 1, 3 clusters of 40, spread 1.6, in the plane. The directions: seed 5, 4 classes of 40, cone 0.6, lengths 0.5 to 2, in three dimensions. The split by seed 11: a quarter held out (30 of the clusters, 40 of the directions), the rest trained on. The network: one hidden layer of 8 tanh units then softmax (51 numbers on the clusters, 68 on the directions), the same seeded starting weights for both representations of a set, Adam at rate 0.02 for 300 steps.",
    "The measures: held-out accuracy for both methods; the training loss by step, the loss at the end and the steps to a loss under 0.5 for the network; the distortion of each representation against its set's own distances, after one scale is fitted (an angle and a length are in different units); and on the way there, the three distances on three pairs, a path and a mean in each space, and the decision boundary of nearest neighbors on a grid.",
    "Written before the run. On the clusters the sphere should cost accuracy, since it throws the positions away and keeps only which way from the origin each point lies: clusters that lie in the same direction at different distances merge. On the directions the sphere should gain a little, as the baseline page's lengths-dropped row already did, and the network may gain less, since a network can learn to ignore the lengths on its own. The distortion of the crossed cells should be large at their worst pair: two points in nearly the same direction at different lengths are far in the flat space and near on the sphere.",
    "Part 2: the tree (depth 4, branching 2) by three spaces at the same dimension, 2: the unit sphere (curvature +1, drawn in three coordinates but a surface of two), the plane (0) and the unit disk (-1). The same start in every space, seeded normal numbers times 0.5 as a tangent vector at the origin of each space carried into it by its own step (the sphere's at the north pole, the disk's at the middle; the same vector, but each space bends it its own way, so the three starts have different distortions, 0.78, 0.78 and 0.66, read off the chart); 200 steps at rate 50 (a rate for a loss that is a mean over 465 pairs; a first run at rates 1 to 200 found 50 the largest the plane's fit stayed stable at); the scale fixed. Three starts (seeds 31, 32, 33) for the spread; the first start's embeddings are drawn. The measures: the mean distortion and the worst pair by step and at the end; the steps to a mean distortion under 0.2 (the target was set at 0.2 after the first run at 150 steps showed the plane's fits settling near 0.15, so that the plane's speed could be read too; the step count was raised from 150 to 200 after that run showed the disk's fits still falling while the plane's had stopped: of the three choices made after seeing a run, the rate favors the plane's stability and the step count the disk, and the analysis says so where it rules); the accuracy of nearest neighbors (k = 3) under the space's own distance and of the logistic classifier (200 steps at rate 0.5) on the held-out quarter of the nodes (7 of 31, split seed 11); the numbers fitted (the coordinates, 2 per node in the plane and the disk, 3 on the sphere, which is a surface of two dimensions drawn in three) and the operations (6 per entry of the 31 by 31 distance matrix per coordinate per step, the diagonal counted); how far past the rim any step went. The control: the same fit from the first start at curvatures +1, +1/4, +1/16, 0, -1/16, -1/4, -1, -4, for 100 steps each, the three main fits read at their step 100 for +1, 0 and -1. All of it is in the preset at the bottom, data the program reads.",
    "Written before the run, for the tree. The disk should keep the tree's distances with the least distortion and the sphere the most, since the tree's levels double and the disk's room doubles with them while the sphere's closes up: the unit sphere's farthest points are pi apart and the tree needs 8, so its distortion should be large. The plane should fall between. The classifiers should tell the two branches apart in every space that keeps the tree at all, since the branches are far apart along the tree. The control should fall from +1 through 0 to some negative curvature and rise again at -4, where the tree is pushed toward the rim.",
];

pub const RUN: [&str; 3] = [
    "The run is the two programs. Part 1: nearest neighbors on each held-out quarter under each distance, and four training runs of the network, one per cell, each 300 steps from the same weights, the training loss kept at every step. Nothing is tuned to the result: the step count, rate and seeds were fixed before the run and are the same for both representations of a set.",
    "Part 2: fourteen fits of the tree, nine for the three spaces by three starts and five more for the control's other curvatures, each a loop of the same step with the mean distortion kept at every step; then nearest neighbors and the logistic classifier on each of the first start's three embeddings. The rate, the step count and the target were set as the experiment frame says, by a first run, and are the same for every space.",
    "The loss curves below are the four runs' training losses by step; the convergence table reads off each curve's first and last values and the first step under 0.5. The held-out accuracies in the record are the network's at the last step, on the same held-out rows nearest neighbors was scored on. Then part 2's sections: the disk, the three embeddings, the fit by step and by start, the control.",
];

pub const ANALYSIS: [&str; 6] = [
    "The record, one row per run, every cell measured. Nearest neighbors: on the flat clusters the sphere costs, 0.90 to 0.77, four more of the 30 held-out points wrong, because the three clusters lie in two directions from the origin and two of them merge on the circle. On the directions the sphere gains one point of 40, 0.75 to 0.775, the baseline's lengths-dropped row again. The network: on the clusters it loses the same way, 0.87 to 0.77; on the directions it comes out the same under both, 0.75, ten of 40 wrong each way, the flat run reaching a training loss of 0.5 at step 203 and the spherical run not reaching it in 300.",
    "What caused each difference. On the clusters it is the lost dimension: a cluster's position is its signal and the sphere keeps only its direction; the boundary picture under the angle shows two clusters sharing a wedge of rays. On the directions, for nearest neighbors, it is the lengths: under the straight line a point far out is far from everything, and a short point is near the short points of other classes; the angle ignores that, and the map's boundary shows it, the straight line's moving with the length and the angle's staying put. For the network the page measures only the outcome: the same held-out accuracy, and a lower training loss under the flat representation. A network with a hidden layer can represent a rule that ignores the lengths, which would be consistent with the tie, but whether this one did is not measured here; nor is why the flat run's loss fell faster. Both are open.",
    "Against H1, by the rule written before the run. The clusters count for it: the matching space scored higher and the crossed one cost, under both methods. The directions under nearest neighbors count for it by one point of 40, which is within what one point can move. The directions under the network are a tie, and the rule said the plane scoring as high as the sphere counts against: so that cell counts against H1, a [null result|null-result] reported as one. The distortion rows say what the crossed spaces lose in distance terms, 0.49 on average for the clusters on the sphere and 0.37 for the directions kept flat, with the worst pair of the directions off by a factor of 79: two points in nearly the same direction at lengths far apart, whose scaled straight-line distance is 79 times their angle.",
    "Part 2, the tree. The disk keeps its distances with the least distortion, 0.10 on average on the first start against the plane's 0.15 and the unit sphere's 0.59; the worst pair is 0.29 in the disk, 0.62 in the plane, 0.95 on the sphere. The column distortion, scale forgiven is part 1's measure applied here: the distortion after one scale is fitted so that the mean distance matches the tree's (not so that the distortion is least, so it can come out above the fixed-scale figure, as it does for every row). The sphere's is 0.60, no lower than its 0.59: fitting a scale to the sphere's embedding does not help, so the sphere's loss is in the shape of its distances (its farthest points pi apart, every distance of the tree squeezed), not in their unit; a larger sphere helps only by letting the fit find another shape, which is what the control's rows at +1/4 and +1/16 show. The two classifiers tell the branches apart in the plane and in the disk alike (nearest neighbors 7 of 7, the logistic classifier 6 of 7) and fail on the sphere (3 of 7 each): the sphere's embedding has folded the branches together. The plane and the disk reached a mean distortion under 0.2 at steps 24 and 29 (the disk from a start 0.12 lower, and still later: its steps near the rim are short); the sphere did not in 200. No step in the disk went past the rim; the sphere and the plane have none. The three starts: the chart's dashed curves say what kind of end each is. The plane's second start is flat from step 50 at 0.30, a local minimum; the disk's second flattens by step 150 at 0.24 and its third is still falling at step 200 (0.14), slow convergence rather than a minimum. On those starts the baseline page's plain layout, no fit, at 0.18, is below both fits' ends: 200 steps from that start ended above a drawing.",
    "The control: from the unit sphere (0.59 at step 100) the distortion falls through +1/4 (0.31), +1/16 (0.18) and the plane (0.15) to -1/16 (0.13) and -1/4 (0.10), is 0.12 at -1 and 0.34 at -4, where the tree is pushed toward the rim. The lowest of the eight is at -1/4, on one start at step 100; the three negative rows from -1/16 to -1 lie within 0.035 of each other, less than the spread over starts, so which negative curvature is lowest is not settled, only that the two sides meet at the plane and that -4 is too much.",
    "Against H1, for the tree, by the rule written before the saga (docs/research-questions.md). On accuracy the disk's is at least the plane's (1.000 and 0.857 each), which that rule reads as for H1; part 1's rule read a tie against, and under that stricter reading the tree's accuracy is a [null result|null-result] too, since the plane does as well. On distortion the disk is under the plane by 0.05 on the first start, and the spread of the disk's over its three starts is 0.13 (the plane's, 0.14): the rule counts the plane within that spread against H1, so by its letter the tree's distortion does not count for H1, a null result reported as one. The spread is the fit's doing (one start of the plane stopped in a local minimum, two of the disk ended high) rather than the geometry's, and the rule was written for starts compared unpaired; paired by start, the disk is under the plane on all three (by 0.05, 0.06 and 0.01), which is the direction H1 says and not what the rule asked for. Three choices were made after seeing a run (the experiment frame says which): the rate, which favors the plane's stability, the target, and the step count, which favors the disk; the ruling on distortion rests on the spread, which none of the three set. What would settle it: more starts, a fit that escapes its local minima (several restarts, or a schedule for the rate), or a larger tree, where the plane's crowding bites by the count above; none of which this page ran.",
];

pub const EXTENSION: [&str; 3] = [
    "What this rests on: the cosine comparison of word vectors and the unit-sphere embeddings of face and sentence models, which drop the length on purpose; and the measure of distortion, used by Nickel and Kiela (2017) and Gu, Sala, Gunel and Re (2019) to compare the spaces a set of distances is embedded in, read at source (docs/papers.md). What remains open here: the spread over seeds (one split, one starting weight set), a network given the length as an extra input beside the direction against one given the direction alone, and the directions with the lengths made to carry a class, where the sphere would lose.",
    "What this page does not establish: that any real data set is directional or flat. The two sets were built to be; a real one's structure is measured, by this page's distortion against a candidate space, not assumed.",
    "Part 2 rests on Nickel and Kiela (2017), whose Poincare embeddings of WordNet showed a hierarchy's links recovered far more fully from a disk of a few dimensions than from a flat space of the same size (they report ranks, not distortion), and on Ganea, Becigneul and Hofmann (2018) for the exponential map and Mobius addition the step uses; the distortion measure and the fixed-scale comparison across curvatures follow Gu, Sala, Gunel and Re (2019), who learn the curvature of each factor of a product space. All three read at source (docs/papers.md). Open here: the clusters and the directions fitted in the disk (two cells of the H1 table); a fit that escapes its local minima, so the spread is the geometry's and not the optimizer's; a larger tree, and trees of branching 3, where the same count (27 nodes on level 3 round a circle of length 18.8) says the plane runs out of room by level 3; and the deception H3 names (H3, the third [hypothesis|hypothesis] of this repository, geometry learned with the model), a space that comes out lower on distortion and lower on the task, which this page did not see (the disk came out lower on distortion and level on accuracy) but was built to be able to see.",
];

